"texte": [
"Solemn Address — Distinguished members of the Academy, educators, historians, and fellow citizens, every human civilisation rests upon an invisible relay race across time: the patient, demanding labour of intergenerational transmission through which one generation hands over to the next not merely material infrastructure, but languages, historical memory, ethical wisdom, artistic traditions, and institutional craft. As Hannah Arendt reminded us in her luminous reflection on the crisis in education, human beings are born into an already existing world that is older than they are, and the decisive responsibility of adults is to introduce newcomers to that inheritance so that they may both inhabit it and renew it. Yet today, across rapidly changing societies, this vital chain of transmission appears to be snapping—caught between an unprecedented acceleration of technological rupture on one side and a spreading tide of collective amnesia on the other.",
"The first source of this breakdown lies in the sheer velocity of socio-technical transformation, which has inverted the traditional authority of experience. Throughout most of history, elders possessed indispensable practical knowledge precisely because the world of the grandchild closely resembled that of the grandparent. In an era governed by exponential digital innovation, artificial intelligence, and shifting ecological baselines, however, accumulated experience is frequently dismissed as obsolete baggage. Young people increasingly navigate cognitive and social landscapes unfamiliar to their parents and teachers, fostering a seductive but dangerous illusion that the past has nothing left to teach the present and that each cohort must invent the world entirely from scratch.",
"Simultaneously, educational institutions and cultural policies have increasingly capitulated to a narrow utilitarianism that measures knowledge solely by its immediate market employability. Under the pressure of constant curriculum reform and metric-driven efficiency, historical depth, classical literature, philosophical inquiry, and civic memory are routinely trimmed away as decorative luxuries. Yet when a society ceases to transmit the complex, tragic, and hard-won lessons of its own history—how democracies collapse, how propaganda seduces, how social rights were wrested from entrenched privilege—it strips younger citizens of the critical immunity required to recognise old tyrannies dressed in modern algorithmic attire.",
"The irony of this cultural amnesia is that it unfolds amidst an unprecedented technological capacity to archive everything. Never has humanity possessed larger digital databases, more searchable libraries, or more ubiquitous recordings of past events; yet information storage is not living transmission. An archive untouched by interpretive dialogue, pedagogical mentorship, and emotional resonance remains a silent warehouse of dead data. True transmission requires living mediators—teachers, elders, artisans, and storytellers—who translate inert facts into lived meaning and moral judgment.",
"Equally troubling is the growing emotional and political rift between generations, fuelled by converging ecological, housing, and fiscal crises. When younger cohorts perceive that older generations have enjoyed decades of stable employment, affordable property, and ecological abundance while bequeathing a destabilised climate, precarious labour markets, and mounting public debt, gratitude gives way to resentment. Intergenerational transmission cannot flourish in an atmosphere of perceived betrayal; young people will scarcely consent to steward a cultural inheritance if they feel excluded from a viable material and planetary future.",
"Nevertheless, repairing this broken relay must never mean imposing a museum-like reverence for the past that stifles youthful creativity or perpetuates historical injustices. Authentic transmission is neither blind repetition nor iconoclastic erasure; it is a critical dialogue in which each generation receives an inheritance, interrogates its blind spots, preserves its hard-won wisdom, and reimagines it for new circumstances. As the poet René Char famously wrote, our inheritance was left to us by no testament—meaning that each epoch must actively claim, interpret, and earn what it receives.",
"Ladies and gentlemen, a society that severs its dialogue with the dead inevitably betrays the unborn. If we wish to overcome both generational rupture and collective amnesia, we must restore dignity to the vocation of teaching, protect historical and humanistic education from short-sighted economism, and honour our material obligation to leave younger generations a habitable planet—so that the bridge between memory and hope may once again bear the weight of human civilisation."
