"titre2": "Exercise 22",
"texte": [
"Strategic Policy Chronicle — For generations, classical diplomacy distinguished sharply between the peaceful persuasion of trade and the coercive pressure of military force. Following the end of the Cold War, liberal internationalists confidently predicted that deepening economic interdependence—woven through global supply chains, integrated financial clearing systems, and cross-border investment—would render coercion obsolete by making conflict prohibitively costly for all parties. Yet the past decade has witnessed a dramatic inversion of that thesis: rather than pacifying great-power rivalry, economic interdependence itself has been transformed into the primary battlefield of coercive diplomacy. Through sweeping financial sanctions, export controls on advanced semiconductors, secondary tariffs, and asset freezes, major powers increasingly seek to compel geopolitical concessions without firing a single shot.",
"The theoretical appeal of economic statecraft is readily apparent to policymakers wary of the catastrophic human and political costs of conventional warfare. By leveraging central nodes in global finance, energy transit, or technological manufacturing—what political scientists Henry Farrell and Abraham Newman have termed 'weaponised interdependence'—a dominant state can inflict severe economic pain upon an adversary while claiming the moral high ground of non-violent pressure. Unlike naval blockades of earlier centuries, contemporary financial and technological sanctions operate silently through banking networks, software licences, and insurance markets, reaching deep into a target economy's industrial lifeblood.",
"Nevertheless, empirical experience reveals that coercive economic diplomacy frequently collides with stubborn strategic paradoxes that blunt its effectiveness. First, comprehensive sanctions rarely produce rapid political capitulation; on the contrary, by inflicting visible hardship upon ordinary populations, external economic pressure often triggers a 'rally-around-the-flag' effect, enabling authoritarian rulers to blame domestic economic failures on foreign hostility while consolidating internal control. Meanwhile, ruling elites routinely insulate themselves through black markets, state subsidies, and illicit networks, leaving the heaviest burden to fall upon vulnerable civilians and middle-class entrepreneurs.",
"Second, in a deeply interconnected global economy, coercive measures rarely remain one-way instruments; they act as boomerangs that wound the sender and destabilise third parties. Restricting energy exports, freezing sovereign reserves, or severing major manufacturing hubs from global supply chains triggers inflationary surges, supply bottlenecks, and financial volatility across neutral and allied economies alike. Far from isolating a target cleanly, aggressive economic coercion often accelerates the fragmentation of global markets as non-aligned nations rush to diversify supply routes and reduce exposure to any single dominant currency or technological chokepoint.",
"The supreme irony of overusing coercive interdependence is that it gradually erodes the very structural advantage upon which it depends. Asymmetric control over global financial networks or critical technologies endures only so long as other nations trust that those infrastructures will function as neutral, predictable public utilities. Once states witness financial clearing systems or semiconductor supply chains being deployed as geopolitical weapons, even friendly or neutral powers begin investing heavily in alternative payment mechanisms, indigenous industrial capacity, and redundant trade corridors—steadily diminishing the coercer's future leverage.",
"None of this implies that economic sanctions or export controls are entirely illegitimate or devoid of strategic utility. Targeted, multilateral measures—such as restricting dual-use military components, freezing the assets of specific human rights violators, or enforcing clear United Nations mandates—can raise the cost of aggression and signal collective resolve when paired with credible diplomatic off-ramps. Coercion fails most dramatically when it degenerates into maximalist economic siege warfare untethered from realistic diplomatic objectives.",
"Ultimately, coercive diplomacy in an interdependent world demands a rare combination of strategic restraint and institutional foresight. Interdependence is neither an automatic guarantee of peace nor a magic wand of bloodless victory; it is a delicate web of mutual vulnerability. States that treat global economic networks purely as instruments of unilateral compulsion risk tearing apart the very fabric of shared prosperity upon which their own long-term security and influence depend."
