"titre": "Economic Warfare as an Instrument of Geopolitical Domination",
"titre2": "Exercise 25",
"texte": [
"Economic and Historical Essay — Though frequently portrayed in contemporary commentary as a bloodless, modern alternative to military conquest, economic warfare is among the oldest and most ruthless instruments of geopolitical domination. From Athens's Megarian Decree starving a rival city-state of maritime trade in the fifth century before the Common Era, to Napoleon's Continental System and the Allied naval blockades of the twentieth century, great powers have consistently sought to subjugate adversaries by severing their access to food, energy, capital, and strategic materials. What distinguishes twenty-first-century economic warfare is not its underlying ambition of compulsion, but its surgical precision, planetary reach, and seamless integration into the everyday plumbing of global finance, digital networks, and industrial supply chains.",
"Modern economic warfare operates through an arsenal of instruments far subtler than warships patrolling a harbour. By weaponising reserve-currency dominance, cutting target banks off from international messaging networks, imposing extraterritorial secondary sanctions that penalise third-country firms for trading with a designated adversary, restricting exports of foundational lithography machines or artificial intelligence chips, and deploying predatory debt-trap financing or strategic resource monopolies, dominant powers can strangle an economy's developmental prospects while maintaining the outward appearance of peacetime legality.",
"The structural asymmetry of this mode of domination lies in how unevenly global economic vulnerabilities are distributed. Nations that control key chokepoints—whether dollar clearing houses, advanced semiconductor fabrication, submarine telecommunications cables, or critical mineral refining—wield an exorbitant privilege: they can project punitive power across borders without deploying a single soldier, while shielding their own domestic populations from immediate physical danger. Conversely, developing and middle-income economies reliant on imported technology, external credit, or commodity exports find their sovereign policy space narrowed by the constant threat of financial exclusion or supply disruption.",
"The bitter irony of economic warfare is that its human toll often rivals or exceeds that of limited military operations, yet its casualties remain largely invisible to international moral accounting. When comprehensive financial blockades prevent hospitals from importing spare medical parts, cripple water-treatment infrastructure, trigger hyperinflation, and destroy middle-class savings, the resulting suffering falls overwhelmingly upon children, the elderly, and the poor—the very citizens least capable of influencing an authoritarian government's foreign policy. By framing economic strangulation as 'peaceful pressure', initiating powers frequently evade the ethical scrutiny that overt military bombardment would immediately provoke.",
"Furthermore, the relentless escalation of economic warfare threatens to undermine the foundational architecture of global prosperity itself. For decades, international economic integration rested on the implicit covenant that trade rules, payment systems, and scientific collaboration would remain insulated from day-to-day geopolitical quarrels. As major powers increasingly subordinate efficiency to national security—through 'friend-shoring', export blacklists, and investment screening—the world economy risks fracturing into rival, high-cost technological and monetary spheres that slow global innovation and penalise poorer nations most severely.",
"Equally dangerous is the dynamic of escalation that economic warfare sets in motion. History warns that when a major power feels cornered by resource embargoes, technological containment, or financial siege—as imperial Japan experienced prior to 1941—economic strangulation can precipitate desperate military gambles rather than peaceful compliance. Far from serving as a guaranteed substitute for war, unrestrained economic coercion can become the very catalyst that ignites armed conflict.",
"Ultimately, restraining economic warfare requires restoring clear legal and ethical boundaries within international economic governance. While states possess legitimate rights to protect vital national security technologies and impose narrowly targeted sanctions against egregious violations of international law, the wholesale weaponisation of global trade, finance, and civilian subsistence constitutes a grave threat to human dignity and world peace. True leadership in an interdependent century demands safeguarding open, rule-bound economic commons rather than turning the arteries of global livelihood into instruments of imperial subjugation."
