"texte": [
"Solemn Address — Excellencies, ambassadors, scholars of international governance, and fellow citizens, whenever humanity has emerged from the ashes of global catastrophe—whether after the Peace of Westphalia, the Napoleonic Wars, or the devastation of 1945—statesmen have turned to multilateralism as the indispensable architecture for taming international anarchy. Grounded in the noble conviction that shared challenges require collective rules, reciprocal restraint, and institutionalised negotiation, multilateral institutions have enabled extraordinary achievements: preventing nuclear conflagration, eradicating smallpox, expanding global trade, and codifying universal human rights. Yet today, from the paralysis of the United Nations Security Council to deadlocked climate summits and weakening world trade tribunals, multilateralism faces a profound crisis of credibility—caught in the permanent structural contradiction between universal collective aspirations and the stubborn primacy of national interests.",
"The root of this contradiction lies embedded in the very design of international organisations. Unlike domestic democratic states, which possess a monopoly on legitimate enforcement and direct accountability to citizens, multilateral institutions are associations of sovereign governments that remain simultaneously the authors, interpreters, and selective enforcers of international rules. Every head of state enters a multilateral chamber carrying a dual, often irreconcilable mandate: on one hand, a moral commitment to global public goods; on the other, a constitutional obligation to defend immediate national security, domestic employment, and electoral survival back home. Whenever those two imperatives collide, short-term national expediency almost invariably prevails over abstract planetary solidarity.",
"This structural tension is acutely magnified by entrenched inequalities of power within multilateral bodies themselves. Institutions conceived in the mid-twentieth century continue to reflect outdated geopolitical hierarchies—most visibly in permanent Security Council vetoes and weighted voting formulas inside international financial institutions. When great powers invoke multilateral rules to constrain weaker states while exercising veto power or unilateral exemptions whenever those same rules inconvenience their own strategic ambitions, double standards corrode institutional legitimacy from within. Rising and developing nations rightly ask whether multilateralism is a genuine community of equal law or merely a convenient instrument for legitimising the privileges of the powerful.",
"Simultaneously, multilateral diplomacy suffers from a painful mismatch between the slow, consensus-driven rhythms of international negotiation and the exponential velocity of contemporary crises. Achieving unanimous agreement among nearly two hundred sovereign states with divergent economic structures, historical grievances, and political regimes often produces lowest-common-denominator declarations—solemn communiqués rich in aspirational rhetoric yet stripped of binding enforcement mechanisms. While diplomats negotiate procedural commas over years, atmospheric carbon concentrations climb, autonomous weapons proliferate, and debt crises devastate vulnerable populations.",
"The subtle irony of this institutional malaise is that retreating into narrow economic nationalism or unilateral power politics offers no escape from interdependence; it merely strips nations of the collective tools needed to manage shared vulnerabilities. No sovereign state, however mighty its military or vast its economy, can unilaterally arrest planetary warming, contain cross-border pandemics, regulate global artificial intelligence, or stabilise international financial contagion. Those who celebrate the weakening of multilateralism in the name of 'national sovereignty' frequently discover that unchecked global anarchy leaves every nation less secure and less sovereign in practice.",
"Rescuing multilateralism from both cynical paralysis and utopian irrelevance therefore requires reforming its architecture to align national self-interest with collective survival. First, global governance must become genuinely representative—reforming the Security Council, democratising global financial governance, and giving fair voice to Africa, Latin America, and Asia so that international rules command authentic global ownership. Second, universal frameworks should be complemented by flexible, action-oriented coalitions of committed states capable of moving swiftly on climate finance, digital governance, and public health without being held hostage by a single obstructionist veto.",
"Ladies and gentlemen, multilateralism was never meant to be a naive denial of national interests; it was conceived as the highest, most far-sighted expression of enlightened self-interest. In an interconnected century where every nation's security is bound up with the stability of the whole, the only durable way to defend the national interest is to build fair, accountable, and effective institutions capable of safeguarding our common human home."
