"Henrik Lindqvist: 'Because digital architectures have transformed both the scale and the psychological mechanics of deception. Traditional propaganda operated largely through centralised broadcasting—attempting to persuade an audience to believe a specific official lie. Contemporary information operations, by contrast, leverage algorithmic amplification, micro-targeted psychographic profiling, synthetic media, and coordinated bot networks not necessarily to convince citizens of one coherent falsehood, but to flood the public sphere with so much contradictory noise, outrage, and conspiracy that people lose confidence in the very possibility of distinguishing truth from fabrication.'",
"Journalist: 'That distinction is striking: you are suggesting that sowing epistemic chaos can be more destabilising than promoting a single ideological narrative.'",
"Henrik Lindqvist: 'Infinitely more destabilising! As political theorists of modern disinformation have observed, when a population is saturated with competing fabrications—simultaneously told that an election was rigged, that public health agencies are lying, that courts are corrupt, and that independent journalists are paid agents—citizens retreat into cynical apathy or tribal paranoia. A fractured, demoralised public unable to agree on basic shared facts becomes incapable of collective self-government, sparing a hostile actor the immense cost of overt military intervention.'",
"Journalist: 'Yet attributing democratic polarisation solely to foreign disinformation campaigns risks overlooking domestic vulnerabilities. To what extent do external actors exploit fractures that societies have already created themselves?'",
"Henrik Lindqvist: 'That is a crucial corrective, and ignoring it leads to dangerous complacency. Foreign influence operations rarely invent social divisions out of thin air; they act like accelerants poured onto existing dry timber—economic inequality, institutional neglect, partisan media sensationalism, and legitimate public grievances that political elites have failed to address. Furthermore, domestic political entrepreneurs frequently adopt the very same manipulative playbooks, laundering conspiratorial narratives through mainstream commentary until external interference and internal polarisation become virtually indistinguishable.'",
"Journalist: 'How can open democracies defend themselves against systematic information manipulation without slipping into state censorship or undermining freedom of expression?'",
"Henrik Lindqvist: 'Heavy-handed government truth ministries or sweeping speech bans are both ineffective and deeply counterproductive, as they validate conspiratorial claims of authoritarian suppression. Effective defence requires focusing on systemic architecture rather than policing individual opinions: enforcing algorithmic transparency so platforms cannot secretly amplify outrage for profit, dismantling opaque automated bot networks, protecting local investigative journalism, and investing early in cognitive and media literacy so citizens learn to recognise emotional manipulation before sharing it.'",
"Journalist: 'In short, resilience against information warfare depends as much on civic and institutional health as on cybersecurity.'",
"Henrik Lindqvist: 'Precisely: information manipulation triumphs only when public trust has already been hollowed out from within. Rebuilding fair institutions, honest political leadership, and an educated, reflective citizenry remains the single most powerful antidote to cognitive destabilisation.'"
