"texte": [
"Solemn Address — Excellencies, distinguished scholars, delegates of civil society, and fellow citizens, we inhabit a perilous historical paradox: never has human civilisation been more deeply interconnected by shared existential risks—planetary climate breakdown, biodiversity collapse, pandemic pathogens, artificial intelligence governance, nuclear proliferation, and volatile financial contagion—and never have the international institutions entrusted with managing those collective perils appeared more enfeebled, gridlocked, and contested. From the paralysed dispute-settlement appellate body of the World Trade Organization to deadlocked United Nations Security Council resolutions and underfunded global health and climate mechanisms, the post-1945 architecture of global governance is suffering a profound crisis of authority and effectiveness.",
"To diagnose this institutional decay honestly, we must look beyond superficial complaints about diplomatic incompetence and examine three converging structural causes. First is the acute mismatch between twentieth-century institutional power structures and twenty-first-century geopolitical realities. International bodies designed in the aftermath of 1945 continue to grant disproportionate voting shares and veto privileges to a narrow circle of victors from a vanished era, while under-representing the demographic, economic, and moral weight of Africa, Asia, and Latin America. Institutions perceived as unrepresentative inevitably suffer a haemorrhaging of legitimacy: excluded nations grow reluctant to enforce rules they had no equitable voice in shaping.",
"Second is the deliberate erosion of multilateral rules by major powers themselves whenever institutional constraints collide with short-term domestic politics or strategic rivalry. When great powers bypass international courts, block judicial appointments to trade tribunals, weaponise global financial clearing systems unilaterally, or treat binding climate pledges as optional aspirations, they teach the entire international community a corrosive lesson: that international law is merely a convenience for the strong rather than an impartial constraint binding all states equally.",
"Third is the inability of state-centric governance models to keep pace with non-state actors and borderless technological transformations. Treaties negotiated exclusively among foreign ministries over decades struggle to regulate transnational technology conglomerates whose artificial intelligence models evolve monthly, private financial flows that cross borders in milliseconds, or non-state armed networks and cyber syndicates operating in legal shadows. Global governance today frequently resembles an eighteenth-century sailing vessel attempting to navigate a twenty-first-century hurricane.",
"Yet the seductive temptation to celebrate the decline of international institutions in the name of unconstrained national sovereignty rests upon a fatal delusion. Dismantling global governance does not restore freedom of action to individual states; it plunges humanity into an ungoverned commons where collective action problems become insoluble. No single nation—however wealthy or heavily armed—can negotiate a bilateral treaty with the atmosphere, erect a border wall against novel viruses, or unilaterally prevent a race to the bottom in corporate taxation and artificial intelligence safety.",
"Reversing this crisis of global governance therefore requires neither nostalgic defence of a broken status quo nor nihilistic abandonment of international law, but a bold, two-pronged institutional renewal. First, existing multilateral pillars must undergo genuine structural democratisation—reforming Security Council governance, overhauling international financial institutions to address sovereign debt and climate justice, and granting emerging and developing regions fair decision-making authority. Second, formal treaties must be complemented by agile, multi-stakeholder governance networks that bring together states, cities, scientific bodies, civil society, and regulated industry under enforceable standards of transparency and accountability.",
"Ladies and gentlemen, international institutions are neither self-sustaining machines nor optional diplomatic ornaments; they are the fragile, hard-won immune system of an interconnected civilisation. If we allow them to atrophy through selfishness, double standards, and short-sighted nationalism, we will discover too late that in a single shared biosphere, the collapse of global governance is indistinguishable from collective self-harm. Let us summon the courage to rebuild institutions worthy of our interdependence—before crisis becomes the sole remaining teacher of human solidarity."
