"texte": [
"Financial and Historical Chronicle — In classical economic thought, finance was conceived as a modest, indispensable servant to the real economy: mobilising household savings, channelling credit toward productive workshops and infrastructure, and insuring merchants against maritime or agricultural risk. Over the past four decades, however, that traditional hierarchy has undergone a historic inversion. Through deregulation of cross-border capital flows, the proliferation of complex derivatives, shareholder-value orthodoxy, and algorithmic high-frequency trading, finance has ceased to be merely an instrument serving material production; it has become an autonomous, self-referential empire whose daily transactions dwarf the trade in actual goods and services by orders of magnitude, creating a widening chasm between paper asset inflation and real societal wealth.",
"The core mechanism of this financialisation lies in the redirection of capital away from long-term productive investment toward short-term speculative extraction. When corporate governance is judged primarily by quarterly stock price appreciation, firms increasingly channel profits into share buybacks, leveraged mergers, and dividend payouts rather than research, worker training, or resilient industrial capacity. Simultaneously, commercial banks have shifted lending away from small and medium-sized productive enterprises toward real-estate mortgages and financial assets, inflating housing bubbles that turn basic shelter into a speculative financial vehicle unaffordable for working families.",
"The structural absurdity of this disconnect became starkly visible during the 2008 global financial crisis and again amid the monetary expansions of the early 2020s: stock indices and luxury asset valuations soared to historic highs even as real wages stagnated, public infrastructure decayed, and supply chains faltered. When financial claims multiply far faster than the underlying capacity of society to produce food, energy, housing, healthcare, and clean technology, 'wealth' on balance sheets becomes largely a claim on future rents extracted from the productive economy.",
"Moreover, hyper-financialisation profoundly distorts the moral and civic fabric of democratic societies. By rewarding speculative arbitrage, tax engineering, and rent-seeking far more lavishly than engineering, nursing, teaching, or manufacturing, it inverts social prestige and diverts some of the brightest mathematical and scientific minds from solving tangible human problems into designing millisecond trading algorithms. Worse still, when private financial institutions grow 'too big to fail', profits are privatised during booms while catastrophic losses are socialised onto taxpayers during inevitable busts—eroding public trust in fairness and democratic accountability.",
"The subtle irony of this financial hypertrophy is that it ultimately undermines the stability of finance itself. Financial assets are not self-sustaining miracles; their long-term value depends entirely on the health, productivity, and purchasing power of the real economy beneath them. By squeezing wages, underinvesting in public goods, and loading households, corporations, and sovereign states with unsustainable debt burdens, unchecked financialisation steadily weakens the very productive base required to honour its own inflated promises.",
"Re-anchoring finance to real wealth does not mean abolishing modern capital markets or retreating to primitive barter; sophisticated credit and risk-sharing mechanisms remain vital for financing the green energy transition and technological innovation. What it demands is restoring clear institutional boundaries: separating commercial deposit banking from speculative investment trading, taxing short-term financial transactions and unearned capital rents, curbing predatory share buybacks, and steering public and private credit toward productive, socially useful, and ecologically regenerative enterprises.",
"Ultimately, money and financial instruments are symbols of trust—maps representing the terrain of human labour, ingenuity, and natural abundance. Whenever a civilisation mistakes the inflation of the map for the enrichment of the territory, it courts both economic fragility and social disillusionment. True prosperity is restored only when finance resumes its rightful vocation: not as the master extracting tribute from society, but as the disciplined servant of real human flourishing."
