    "texte": [
      "Political and Economic Essay — When Justice Louis Brandeis famously warned a century ago that a society may have concentrated wealth in the hands of a few or it may have democracy, but it cannot have both, he articulated a structural truth that contemporary capitalism has tested to the breaking point. Over the past four decades, wave after wave of corporate consolidation, network-effect digital monopolies, asset-management oligopolies, and extreme personal wealth accumulation have produced levels of economic concentration unseen since the Gilded Age. While constitutional democracies continue to celebrate the formal equality of 'one person, one vote' on election day, the silent concentration of economic power increasingly determines which policies are conceivable, which candidates are viable, and which public grievances never reach the legislative agenda.",
      "The first channel through which concentrated wealth subverts democratic equality lies in the direct architecture of political influence. Through lavish campaign financing, armies of corporate lobbyists, funded policy think tanks, and revolving doors connecting regulatory agencies with executive boardrooms, concentrated economic actors translate financial dominance into disproportionate legislative sway. Empirical studies across mature democracies consistently reveal a sobering asymmetry: when the policy preferences of affluent donors and corporate coalitions diverge from those of ordinary citizens, legislative outcomes align overwhelmingly with the former, reducing formal electoral representation to an increasingly hollow ritual.",
      "Equally consequential, yet far less visible than overt lobbying, is what political economists term the 'structural power of capital'. Even without writing a single cheque to a politician, giant conglomerates, institutional investors, and dominant digital platforms exercise a silent veto over elected governments simply by controlling investment, employment, credit ratings, and critical infrastructure. Whenever a parliament contemplates progressive wealth taxation, strict antitrust enforcement, or ambitious environmental regulation, concentrated economic actors can threaten capital flight, plant closures, or market turmoil—disciplining democratic majorities before a bill is even drafted.",
      "Simultaneously, extreme economic concentration has reshaped the very epistemic infrastructure upon which democratic deliberation depends. Across numerous democracies, legacy newspapers, television networks, and dominant social media platforms have fallen under the ownership or algorithmic control of a tiny circle of billionaires and megacorporations. When the gatekeepers of public attention possess vast commercial and ideological stakes in preserving low taxes, weak labour laws, and light-touch regulation, the boundaries of 'respectable' public debate quietly narrow, marginalising systemic critiques while amplifying narratives favourable to entrenched privilege.",
      "The bitter irony of this oligarchic drift is that it also fuels the very populist backlash that threatens constitutional norms from another angle. When working- and middle-class citizens perceive that democratic institutions respond effortlessly to corporate bailouts and tax cuts while remaining chronically paralysed before stagnant wages, unaffordable housing, and decaying public services, civic alienation deepens. Demagogues readily exploit that justified frustration, redirecting popular anger away from concentrated economic power toward vulnerable minorities, immigrants, or democratic checks and balances themselves.",
      "Reversing this erosion of democracy does not require abolishing private enterprise or stifling genuine entrepreneurial initiative; competitive markets themselves wither when captured by monopolies. What it demands is reviving the republican antimonopoly tradition—enforcing vigorous antitrust breakups of dominant conglomerates, instituting strict public financing of elections and transparent lobbying registries, safeguarding independent public-interest journalism, strengthening trade unions as democratic counterweights, and taxing extreme wealth concentration so that no private fortune can rival the sovereignty of the public.",
      "Ultimately, democracy is more than a periodic casting of ballots; it is a shared civic commitment to political equality among self-governing citizens. Whenever economic power becomes so vast that a handful of private actors can bend laws, shape public perception, and intimidate elected parliaments, citizenship degenerates into nominal subjecthood. Preserving free self-government in the twenty-first century requires remembering that dispersing concentrated economic power is not an assault on liberty—it is the indispensable condition of liberty for everyone else."
