"texte": [
"In-Depth Interview — Interview with Professor Mateo Alvarez, international tax economist and advisor on global fiscal governance — Journalist: 'Professor Alvarez, while ordinary wage earners and local small businesses pay taxes automatically where they live and work, some of the world's most profitable multinational corporations routinely report effective tax rates in the low single digits. How have global enterprises managed to decouple where they generate real economic value from where they declare taxable profits?'",
"Mateo Alvarez: 'They exploit a profound structural mismatch between a twenty-first-century borderless digital and intangible economy and a twentieth-century international tax architecture designed for brick-and-mortar factories. Under traditional rules drafted in the 1920s, multinational subsidiaries are treated as separate legal entities transacting at arm's length. By locating high-value patents, software algorithms, trademarks, and intra-group financing hubs inside zero- or low-tax jurisdictions, corporations charge massive royalty fees and interest payments to their own subsidiaries in high-tax countries—legally shifting hundreds of billions of dollars in profits on paper to tax havens where they employ scarcely a handful of staff.'",
"Journalist: 'Defenders of corporate tax planning frequently argue that as long as firms obey the letter of national tax codes, aggressive optimisation is simply a fiduciary duty to maximise shareholder returns rather than a moral failing.'",
"Mateo Alvarez: 'That legalistic defence confuses formal compliance with ethical and civic legitimacy. First, the boundary between lawful avoidance and unlawful evasion is often razor-thin, maintained by armies of tax lawyers exploiting loopholes that those very corporations lobbied parliaments to preserve. Second, every multinational corporation relies heavily upon publicly funded infrastructure—educated workforces, public courts enforcing intellectual property, fibre-optic networks, and stable legal systems—in the countries where its customers and engineers reside. Free-riding on public goods while shifting tax contributions offshore amounts to a silent subsidy from ordinary taxpayers to global shareholders.'",
"Journalist: 'Why have individual sovereign governments struggled for so long to close these loopholes on their own?'",
"Mateo Alvarez: 'Because of a classic collective-action trap: international tax competition. Whenever one country attempts unilaterally to raise corporate taxes or tax digital revenues, multinational firms threaten to relocate regional headquarters or investments to neighbouring jurisdictions offering sweetheart tax rulings. Over the past four decades, this race to the bottom has driven statutory corporate tax rates down dramatically across the globe, forcing governments either to raise regressive consumption and payroll taxes on working families or to cut vital investments in education, healthcare, and green infrastructure.'",
"Journalist: 'Recent multilateral initiatives—such as the global minimum corporate tax framework negotiated under the OECD and G20—have been hailed as a historic breakthrough. Are these reforms genuinely sufficient to end profit shifting?'",
"Mateo Alvarez: 'Establishing the principle of a global minimum tax is certainly a landmark diplomatic step away from pure tax anarchy, yet the current architecture suffers from serious compromises. A fifteen percent minimum floor remains modest compared to what ordinary workers pay; numerous carve-outs allow large conglomerates to shield substantial profits; and the allocation of taxing rights still favours wealthy headquarter nations over developing countries where natural resources are extracted and digital users generate value. Without stronger, genuinely inclusive rules negotiated under United Nations auspices, developing economies continue to lose tens of billions annually that could fund schools and hospitals.'",
"Journalist: 'What would a truly fair and durable international tax system look like in the decades ahead?'",
"Mateo Alvarez: 'It must treat multinational corporations as single unitary enterprises rather than fictional webs of shell subsidiaries—apportioning global profits fairly according to real economic presence such as sales, employees, and physical assets in each country. Paired with a higher, loophole-free global minimum tax, public country-by-country financial reporting, and progressive taxation of ultra-high-net-worth individuals, international taxation can finally restore fiscal justice and democratic sovereignty in a globalised economy.'"
