"titre": "Can Economic Prosperity Be Conceived Without Material Growth?",
"titre2": "Exercise 40",
"texte": [
"Solemn Address — Distinguished economists, environmental scientists, policymakers, and fellow citizens, for more than seventy years, a single statistical number—the annual percentage growth of gross domestic product—has reigned as the supreme oracle of modern political life. Governments rise and fall on its fluctuations; international credit ratings bow to its trajectory; and human well-being itself is routinely equated with ever-expanding flows of material production and consumption. Yet as planetary boundaries buckle under accelerating climate disruption, biodiversity loss, and resource depletion, while affluent nations simultaneously suffer rising inequality, loneliness, and burnout despite unprecedented material output, a foundational question confronts twenty-first-century economic thought: can genuine human prosperity be conceived, organised, and sustained without perpetual material growth?",
"To answer this question without ideological caricature, we must first rescue the word 'prosperity' from its narrow reduction to quantitative accumulation. In its classical philosophical and Latin roots, prosperity—pro-sperare—signifies flourishing in accordance with hope: living a dignified, secure, creative, and socially connected life within a thriving natural world. Once an economy has satisfied fundamental material needs—nutritious food, clean water, safe shelter, reliable energy, healthcare, and education—further increases in physical throughput yield sharply diminishing returns for human happiness, and eventually turn actively destructive when they erode the ecological and social foundations of life.",
"Critics of post-growth economics immediately raise a formidable practical objection: within contemporary capitalist institutions, halting aggregate growth does not produce serene stability; it triggers recession, rising unemployment, sovereign debt crises, and collapsing tax revenues for public welfare. That objection identifies a real structural addiction, yet it confuses an institutional design flaw with an eternal law of nature. Modern economies are currently engineered like bicycles that topple over unless pedalled ever faster because private debt creation, shareholder profit expectations, and employment models presuppose continuous expansion. Overcoming that dependency requires redesigning economic institutions so they can thrive in dynamic equilibrium rather than compulsive acceleration.",
"What would an architecture of prosperity without material growth look like in practice? First, it distinguishes sharply between destructive quantitative throughput and qualitative human development. Even if physical resource extraction and carbon emissions in affluent nations contract toward sustainable planetary levels, society can expand endlessly in knowledge, artistic creation, scientific discovery, preventive healthcare, ecological restoration, lifelong education, and community care—activities rich in human meaning and employment yet light in material footprint.",
"Second, decoupling human flourishing from endless material expansion requires bold distributional and institutional transformation. When the economic pie is no longer growing indefinitely, social justice can no longer be postponed with promises of future trickle-down abundance; it must be achieved directly through fair predistribution and progressive taxation—curbing extreme wealth concentration, guaranteeing universal basic services such as public transit, healthcare, housing, and childcare, and reducing standard working hours as productivity rises so that technological gains liberate human time rather than fuel overconsumption.",
"Third, conceiving prosperity beyond growth demands global solidarity and differentiated responsibility. While affluent, over-consuming economies must urgently reduce their excessive material and carbon footprints, low- and middle-income nations still require targeted material investment to build clean energy grids, hospitals, schools, and resilient infrastructure. True global justice means contracting wasteful luxury throughput in the Global North precisely to free ecological space for dignified development across the Global South.",
"Ladies and gentlemen, abandoning the dogma of infinite material growth is neither a counsel of despair nor an invitation to austerity; it is an invitation to grow up as a civilisation. An economy wise enough to value clean rivers, unhurried time, secure communities, meaningful work, and a stable climate above endless commodity accumulation is not poorer—it is immeasurably richer. Let us summon the courage to replace the blind worship of 'more' with the mature, humane art of 'enough'."
