"titre": "Can Human Wisdom Keep Pace with Exponential Technological Power?",
"titre2": "Exercise 50",
"texte": [
"Solemn Address — Distinguished scientists, philosophers, jurists, educators, and fellow citizens, we stand at an extraordinary threshold in the long evolutionary story of our species. Within a few brief generations, human ingenuity has unlocked powers that ancient mythologies attributed solely to the gods: splitting the atomic nucleus, editing the genetic code of living organisms, engineering planetary climate systems, and creating artificial intelligences capable of surpassing human performance across widening domains of cognition. Yet as the curve of technological capability rises exponentially, a sobering question echoes across every laboratory, parliament, and household on Earth: can human wisdom—our capacity for moral judgment, self-restraint, long-term foresight, and compassion—keep pace with our accelerating power to reshape or destroy the world?",
"To confront this question honestly, we must first acknowledge a fundamental asymmetry in how science and wisdom develop. Scientific and technological knowledge is cumulative and exponential: each generation of researchers stands upon the shoulders of its predecessors, inheriting validated equations, refined instruments, and vast digital codebases without needing to reinvent calculus or semiconductor physics from scratch. Moral wisdom, by contrast, is neither cumulative nor automatically inherited in our genes or machines: every human being is born ethically unfinished and must learn anew—through culture, education, empathy, and hard-won experience—how to master fear, pride, greed, and tribal hostility.",
"This developmental gap—what the philosopher Günther Anders incisively termed the 'Promethean discrepancy'—grows perilous whenever institutional incentives reward speed and capability over reflection and restraint. In a global economy governed by fierce commercial competition and geopolitical rivalry, engineers, corporations, and states face relentless pressure to build and deploy powerful systems first and ask ethical questions later. When 'move fast and break things' migrates from trivial consumer software into synthetic biology, autonomous weaponry, climate geo-engineering, or foundational artificial intelligence, breaking things ceases to be a playful slogan and becomes an existential hazard.",
"Yet despairing fatalism—the belief that humanity is biologically doomed to destroy itself through cleverness without wisdom—is both historically inaccurate and morally irresponsible. Across the twentieth and twenty-first centuries, humanity has repeatedly demonstrated that when confronted clearly with existential peril, collective wisdom can forge binding guardrails: nuclear non-proliferation treaties and arms-control hotlines that prevented atomic catastrophe during the Cold War, the Montreal Protocol that healed the stratospheric ozone layer, international bans on biological and chemical weapons, and global conventions prohibiting human reproductive cloning.",
"Building a civilization whose wisdom matches its technological reach requires elevating ethical governance from an afterthought to the core architecture of innovation. First, we must embed anticipatory precaution and democratic accountability into emerging technologies—requiring rigorous independent safety evaluations, international verification treaties for frontier artificial intelligence and biosecurity, and enforceable liability rules ensuring that power is never divorced from responsibility.",
"Second, we must transform education to heal the artificial divorce between technical mastery and humanistic wisdom. Training brilliant engineers, computer scientists, and molecular biologists without grounding them in history, ethics, ecology, and philosophy produces formidable specialists blind to the human consequences of their creations—just as educating humanists ignorant of science leaves citizens unable to govern a technological civilisation. Future generations need integrated fluency in both the 'how' of capability and the 'why' of human flourishing.",
"Ladies and gentlemen, technology is neither our saviour nor our executioner; it is an amplifier of human intention. Whether exponential power becomes an instrument of ecological healing, shared abundance, and deeper human dignity—or the author of irreversible ruin—will not be decided by algorithms or equations. It will be decided by whether we summon the humility to recognise our limits, the solidarity to govern our inventions together, and the timeless courage to place wisdom firmly at the helm of power."
